  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "What Happens to PDF Metadata When You Use ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude to Write a Document",
    "datePublished": "2026-10-04",
    "dateModified": "2026-10-04",
    "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel"},
    "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "FileIntel", "url": "https://fileintel.me"},
    "image": "https://fileintel.me/images/pdf-metadata-mismatch.jpg",
    "articleSection": "PDF Metadata and AI",
    "keywords": "AI-generated PDF, PDF metadata, ChatGPT PDF, Gemini PDF, Claude PDF",
    "description": "We exported real AI-drafted text to PDF and inspected the raw metadata. Here's exactly what Word, Google Docs, and PDF export tools do and don't reveal.",
    "url": "https://fileintel.me/blog/ai-generated-pdf-metadata",
    "mainEntityOfPage": {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://fileintel.me/blog/ai-generated-pdf-metadata"}
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude put their name in a PDF's metadata?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No, not when you paste their output into Word, Google Docs, or a similar editor and export to PDF. The Creator and Producer fields in the resulting file name the application that performed the export, such as Microsoft Word or Google Docs, not the AI tool that originally drafted the text. That information simply isn't passed along in a standard copy and paste."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can my professor or boss tell I used AI from the PDF properties?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Not reliably from the metadata fields alone. The Author field reflects whoever's copy of Word or Google Docs created the file, which will typically still show a real person's name regardless of who or what wrote the words. Some circumstantial signals, like a very low revision count or a creation date identical to the modification date, can hint at a paste-and-export workflow, but none of this amounts to proof."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What does the Producer field in a PDF actually mean?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The Producer field names the software library or application that generated the PDF file itself, as opposed to the Creator field, which usually names the originating document application. For a file exported from Word, both fields typically point back to Word. For a file exported from Chrome or Google Docs, both point to Google's Skia PDF rendering engine. Neither field has any concept of what AI tool, if any, was used to write the underlying text."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does the EU AI Act require AI-generated PDFs to be labeled?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Article 50 of the EU AI Act, which became applicable on August 2, 2026, requires certain AI-generated content to carry machine-readable disclosure. In practice this is being implemented through the C2PA Content Credentials standard, whose 2026 specification update added support for marking plain text. However, the standard's recommended delivery method for text is an HTTP header or a separate sidecar file, neither of which typically survives a copy and paste into Word followed by a PDF export, so this labeling does not currently reach that specific workflow by default."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can revision count prove a document was written by AI?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. A low revision count is only a weak, circumstantial signal, not proof of anything. A document pasted once and exported immediately will show a low revision number, but so will a short human-written document that was drafted in one sitting and saved only once. Treat this as one data point among many, never as a standalone conclusion."}
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does copying text from ChatGPT into Word change the Author field?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. The Author field, and the related last-modified-by field, are controlled entirely by the Word application's own configured user name, typically pulled from the signed-in Microsoft account. Pasting text from any source, AI or otherwise, has no effect on that field."}
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json">
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "HowTo",
    "name": "How to check what a document's metadata actually reveals about its origin",
    "totalTime": "PT4M",
    "step": [
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Check the Producer and Creator fields first", "text": "These name the software that exported the PDF, such as Word or Google Docs, never the AI tool that may have drafted the underlying text."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Compare the creation date to the modification date", "text": "A gap of seconds between the two can suggest a document was pasted in and exported in one sitting, though this alone proves nothing."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Look at the revision count if it's available", "text": "A very low count is a weak circumstantial signal worth noting, not a conclusion to act on by itself."},
      {"@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "Treat metadata as one input among several", "text": "Combine it with context, a direct conversation, or an organization's own disclosure policy rather than relying on file properties as a verdict."}
    ]
  }
  </script>

