    <h2 id="section-6">#6. What This Means If You're on Either Side of the Question</h2>
    <p>If you're the one using AI assistance and want to disclose it honestly, don't count on the file to do that work for you. Say so directly in the document's own visible text, or use the Keywords or Subject metadata field deliberately to note AI involvement, since nothing in the ordinary export pipeline will add that disclosure automatically, regulation or no regulation.</p>
    <p>If you're on the other side, trying to figure out whether something you received was AI-drafted, treat metadata as one weak input rather than a verdict. A clean Producer field naming Microsoft Word tells you precisely nothing about who or what wrote the words inside it. A suspiciously low revision count is worth noticing, not acting on by itself. The far more reliable approach, in any setting where it actually matters, legal, academic, or editorial, is a clear disclosure policy that asks people directly, rather than a forensic scavenger hunt through file properties that were never built to answer the question in the first place.</p>

    <div class="callout green">
      <div class="callout-label">Best practice</div>
      <p>If AI disclosure matters in your context, build it into your process rather than your file properties. A simple line in the document itself, a submission checkbox, or a team policy that asks the question outright will always be more reliable than anything a metadata field can currently provide.</p>
    </div>

