    <div class="faq-section">
      <h2 class="faq-section-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude put their name in a PDF's metadata?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No, not when you paste their output into Word, Google Docs, or a similar editor and export to PDF. The Creator and Producer fields in the resulting file name the application that performed the export, such as Microsoft Word or Google Docs, not the AI tool that originally drafted the text. That information simply isn't passed along in a standard copy and paste.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can my professor or boss tell I used AI from the PDF properties?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Not reliably from the metadata fields alone. The Author field reflects whoever's copy of Word or Google Docs created the file, which will typically still show a real person's name regardless of who or what wrote the words. Some circumstantial signals, like a very low revision count or a creation date identical to the modification date, can hint at a paste-and-export workflow, but none of this amounts to proof.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          What does the Producer field in a PDF actually mean?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">The Producer field names the software library or application that generated the PDF file itself, as opposed to the Creator field, which usually names the originating document application. For a file exported from Word, both fields typically point back to Word. For a file exported from Chrome or Google Docs, both point to Google's Skia PDF rendering engine. Neither field has any concept of what AI tool, if any, was used to write the underlying text.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does the EU AI Act require AI-generated PDFs to be labeled?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">Article 50 of the EU AI Act, which became applicable on August 2, 2026, requires certain AI-generated content to carry machine-readable disclosure. In practice this is being implemented through the C2PA Content Credentials standard, whose 2026 specification update added support for marking plain text. However, the standard's recommended delivery method for text is an HTTP header or a separate sidecar file, neither of which typically survives a copy and paste into Word followed by a PDF export, so this labeling does not currently reach that specific workflow by default.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Can revision count prove a document was written by AI?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No. A low revision count is only a weak, circumstantial signal, not proof of anything. A document pasted once and exported immediately will show a low revision number, but so will a short human-written document that was drafted in one sitting and saved only once. Treat this as one data point among many, never as a standalone conclusion.</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <button class="faq-question">
          Does copying text from ChatGPT into Word change the Author field?
          <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg>
        </button>
        <div class="faq-answer"><div class="faq-answer-inner">No. The Author field, and the related last-modified-by field, are controlled entirely by the Word application's own configured user name, typically pulled from the signed-in Microsoft account. Pasting text from any source, AI or otherwise, has no effect on that field.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Limitations</h3>
    <p>Our test used LibreOffice Writer rather than licensed Microsoft Word to perform the document-to-PDF export, chosen for transparency and reproducibility. The underlying mechanism, an office application writing its own name into the Creator and Producer fields during export, is identical in principle to Microsoft Word, which publicly documented sample files confirm writes a string such as "Microsoft® Word for Microsoft 365" into those same fields rather than "LibreOffice" or "Writer." We did not have access to test the native PDF export features built directly into ChatGPT, Gemini, or Claude's own interfaces, and cannot independently confirm the exact Producer string those specific export paths produce, only that the same underlying principle, the field naming the exporting software rather than the content's origin, applies to every PDF export mechanism we are aware of. The regulatory and technical landscape around AI content provenance, including the EU AI Act and the C2PA specification, is evolving quickly, and details described here may change after publication.</p>

    <p class="legal-note">This article is provided for general informational purposes and does not constitute legal advice regarding AI disclosure obligations, academic integrity policy, or regulatory compliance. Consult your institution's policy or qualified counsel for guidance specific to your situation.</p>

